<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Traprain Law</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Traprain Law is a hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-lothian">East Lothian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, crowned by one of the largest hillforts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. A centre of the Votadini from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> through the Roman period, it received Roman goods in quantity, and in 1919 excavators found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traprain-law-treasure">Traprain Law Treasure</a>, a hoard of cut Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> buried in the early 5th century. It was largely abandoned afterwards, as power shifted to sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/traprain-law.jpg" alt="Traprain Law" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Danieljksn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traprain_Law_Aerial.webm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
